# Trivy suppressions for the kube-bench scanner Job ONLY.
#
# SCOPING BY SCAN TARGET, NOT BY SYNTAX
# -------------------------------------
# This file is passed with --ignorefile ONLY when scanning k8s/compliance/,
# which contains nothing but the kube-bench Job. k8s/hardening/ — and any
# future application workload — is scanned with NO ignore file at all, so
# these checks still fail the build everywhere else. The isolation comes
# from the scan invocation, which is unambiguous, rather than from per-entry
# path syntax.
#
# The scanner is NEVER weakened: the scan still runs
# --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --exit-code 1.
#
# Reviewed: 2026-09-19

# ===========================================================================
# WHY A CIS NODE SCANNER CANNOT SATISFY POD SECURITY CHECKS
# ===========================================================================
# kube-bench audits NODE-level CIS controls: kubelet process arguments,
# kubelet config files, systemd units and certificate permissions on the
# host. Performing that audit requires host access:
#
#   hostPID        -> kubelet process arguments are only visible in the host
#                     PID namespace; CIS 4.1.x / 4.2.x check exactly those
#                     arguments.
#   hostPath       -> the benchmark reads files ON THE NODE
#                     (/var/lib/kubelet/config.json, /etc/kubernetes/*,
#                     systemd unit files). They do not exist in a container
#                     filesystem.
#   runAsUser: 0   -> those files are root-owned with 0600/0644 modes.
#
# A kube-bench pod that PASSED these checks could not perform the scan at
# all — it would report every node control as unreadable. These privileges
# are the scanner's function, not an oversight.
#
# MINIMISED, not waived. The Job sets:
#   privileged: false, allowPrivilegeEscalation: false,
#   readOnlyRootFilesystem: true, capabilities drop ALL,
#   seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault (pod + container),
#   every hostPath mount readOnly: true.
#
# BLAST RADIUS CONTAINED BY ISOLATION:
#   * runs in its own 'kube-bench' namespace labelled PSA 'privileged',
#     while every workload namespace stays PSA 'restricted';
#   * its ServiceAccount has read-only RBAC (get/list, no write verbs);
#   * it is a Job, not a Deployment — it exits when the scan completes;
#   * ttlSecondsAfterFinished removes it an hour later.
#
# FIXED RATHER THAN SUPPRESSED:
#   KSV-0030 (seccompProfile) — now RuntimeDefault at pod and container
#   level. It cost the scan nothing, so it was corrected, not ignored.
# ===========================================================================

# KSV-0010 (HIGH) — hostPID: true.
# Required to read kubelet process arguments.
KSV-0010

# KSV-0118 (HIGH) — "default security context allows root privileges".
# Triggered by runAsUser: 0, required to read root-owned kubelet config.
# The context is NOT actually default: privileged=false,
# allowPrivilegeEscalation=false, readOnlyRootFilesystem=true, all
# capabilities dropped, seccompProfile=RuntimeDefault.
KSV-0118

# KSV-0012 (MEDIUM) — runAsNonRoot not set to true.
# Same cause as KSV-0118: the benchmark reads root-owned files.
KSV-0012

# KSV-0105 — runAsUser should be greater than 0.
# Same cause as KSV-0118.
KSV-0105

# KSV-0023 (MEDIUM) — hostPath volumes forbidden.
# The benchmark audits files on the node itself. All three mounts are
# readOnly: true, so the scanner cannot modify what it audits.
KSV-0023
